' ============================================================================
' 41 The scope rule on the Gaussian kernel of the paper.
'
' Source of Figure TL6 in ../tensor-logic/static-semantics.md (section 3).
' The figure applies the rule of section 3.2 of that document to
' K[q, i] = exp(-(Xq[q, j] - Xs[i, j]) ** 2 / Var) (Domingos 2025, p. 11).
' It adds no new fact.
'
' Reading order:
'   1. The tree is the parse of the right side, from the call of exp at the
'      root down to the two accesses that hold the index j.
'   2. Candidate nodes (products and call arguments) have the color of the
'      TL engine. The base of ** is not a candidate.
'   3. scope(j) is the deepest candidate that holds both occurrences of j:
'      the product node. The sum over j sits there, outside the square.
'   4. The spine extends from exp(...) to the product and stops there, because
'      the product has two factors that are not constant. scope(j) is on the
'      spine, so the operator of the equation (sum) aggregates j there.
'
' Colors come from concept-colors.iuml (aliases of the shared palette).
' Render: ./render.sh 41-tl-scope-rule.puml   Check: ./render.sh --check

@startuml 41-tl-scope-rule
!include ../../../casper/theory/diagrams/palette.iuml
!include concept-colors.iuml
$ca_base_skin()
skinparam ArrowColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.2
skinparam RectangleBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam NoteBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBackgroundColor $ca_note_grey
skinparam ranksep 35
skinparam rectangle<<scope>> {
  BorderColor $rrs_effect
  BorderThickness 3
}
hide stereotype
title The scope rule on K[q, i] = exp(-(Xq[q, j] - Xs[i, j]) ** 2 / Var)

rectangle ROOT $rrs_tl_engine [
**exp(…)**, the root of the right side
a candidate: the root counts
as a call argument
]
rectangle PROD <<scope>> $rrs_tl_engine [
**the argument of exp**: the product
(-(…) ** 2) · recip(Var)
a candidate, and **scope(j)**:
the sum over j sits here
<latex>\exp\big(-\sum_{j} (Xq_{qj} - Xs_{ij})^{2} / \mathit{Var}\big)</latex>
]
rectangle NEG $rrs_tl_term [
**unary minus**
not a candidate
]
rectangle POW $rrs_tl_term [
<b>the power</b> (…) ~*~* 2
a constant exponent, and
its base is not a candidate
]
rectangle DIFF $rrs_tl_term [
**the base**: Xq[q, j] - Xs[i, j]
not a candidate
]
rectangle XQ $rrs_storage [
**Xq[q, j]**
an occurrence of j
]
rectangle XS $rrs_storage [
**Xs[i, j]**
an occurrence of j
]
rectangle RECIP $rrs_tl_engine [
**the argument of recip**
a candidate (the reciprocal of /)
]
rectangle VAR $rrs_storage [
**Var**
]

ROOT --> PROD
PROD --> NEG
PROD --> RECIP
NEG --> POW
POW --> DIFF
DIFF --> XQ
DIFF --> XS
RECIP --> VAR

note right of PROD
  The spine extends from exp(…) to this product
  and stops here: the product has two factors
  that are not constant. scope(j) is on the
  spine, so the operator of the equation,
  sum, aggregates j here.
end note

legend bottom
**Concept colors**
|<$rrs_tl_engine> | a candidate node of the scope rule |
|<$rrs_effect>    | the bold border of scope(j): the deepest candidate that holds every occurrence of j |
|<$rrs_tl_term>   | a node that is not a candidate |
|<$rrs_storage>   | an access to an input tensor |
endlegend
@enduml
